A revision now replies to the first version of its series, so every
version and its review share one thread. The superseded folder was
there to retire an old version and point at its successor when the
two lived in separate threads. With one thread, filing the old
version would carry the new one with it, and the successor is
already in view.

There is no superseded tag in b/dev; the tag came only from the
unmerged notmuch series, which this does not touch. What b/dev has is
mail-file's superseded folder. This removes it and the two things
that lean on it:

1. The Emacs "s" key in the filing map, removed first so no binding
   ever calls a verb that is gone.
2. The superseded verb in mail-file, with its man page section and
   test. mail-file no longer delivers mail or needs jq.
3. The folder:superseded term in the patch-triage skill's query.

Testing: the four remaining mail-file atf tests pass, "mail-file
superseded thread:x" now fails with "unknown folder", mandoc -Tlint
reports nothing new, and pm-notmuch.el passes check-parens. Patch 3
merges cleanly with "[PATCH 0/3] Thread each revision under its first
version", which edits nearby lines of the same skill.

